  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": { "en": "Regular Verb Conjugation Pattern", "de": "Regelmäßige Verbkonjugation" },
      "notes": [
        "Remove the infinitive ending -en to find the stem (lernen → lern-).",
        "Add personal endings: ich -e, du -st, er/sie/es -t, wir -en, ihr -t, sie/Sie -en.",
        "The stem NEVER changes in regular verbs."
      ],
      "table": {
        "headers": ["Pronoun", "Ending", "wohnen", "lernen", "machen", "trinken"],
        "rows": [
          ["ich", "-e", "wohne", "lerne", "mache", "trinke"],
          ["du", "-st", "wohnst", "lernst", "machst", "trinkst"],
          ["er/sie/es", "-t", "wohnt", "lernt", "macht", "trinkt"],
          ["wir", "-en", "wohnen", "lernen", "machen", "trinken"],
          ["ihr", "-t", "wohnt", "lernt", "macht", "trinkt"],
          ["sie/Sie", "-en", "wohnen", "lernen", "machen", "trinken"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": { "en": "Stem-Changing Verbs (Strong Verbs)", "de": "Stammwechsel-Verben (Starke Verben)" },
      "notes": [
        "Vowel changes occur ONLY in du and er/sie/es forms. Ich, wir, ihr, sie/Sie stay regular.",
        "Three patterns: e → i, e → ie, a → ä.",
        "Essential verbs to memorize: geben, lesen, essen, fahren, schlafen, sehen."
      ],
      "table": {
        "headers": ["Pattern", "Infinitive", "ich", "du", "er/sie/es", "wir", "ihr", "sie/Sie"],
        "rows": [
          ["e → i", "geben", "gebe", "gibst", "gibt", "geben", "gebt", "geben"],
          ["e → ie", "lesen", "lese", "liest", "liest", "lesen", "lest", "lesen"],
          ["e → ie", "sehen", "sehe", "siehst", "sieht", "sehen", "seht", "sehen"],
          ["e → i", "essen", "esse", "isst", "isst", "essen", "esst", "essen"],
          ["a → ä", "fahren", "fahre", "fährst", "fährt", "fahren", "fahrt", "fahren"],
          ["a → ä", "schlafen", "schlafe", "schläfst", "schläft", "schlafen", "schlaft", "schlafen"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": { "en": "Irregular Verbs: sein & haben", "de": "Unregelmäßige Verben: sein & haben" },
      "notes": [
        "sein is completely irregular — memorize all 6 forms.",
        "haben is irregular in du (hast) and er/sie/es (hat) — drop the 'b' from stem.",
        "These are the two most important verbs in German."
      ],
      "table": {
        "headers": ["Pronoun", "sein (to be)", "haben (to have)"],
        "rows": [
          ["ich", "bin", "habe"],
          ["du", "bist", "hast"],
          ["er/sie/es", "ist", "hat"],
          ["wir", "sind", "haben"],
          ["ihr", "seid", "habt"],
          ["sie/Sie", "sind", "haben"]
        ]
      }
    }
